{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s initial policy of reducing penalties for possessing small quantities of controlled substances will end this weekend under recently passed legislation, which reinstates penalties for such offenses. The new law, HB4002, now allows individuals caught with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine to face either criminal charges or an alternative path involving rehabilitation and behavioral health programs instead of fines. Possession for personal use will now be classified as a misdemeanor, carrying potential jail time of up to half a year. The measure also strengthens enforcement against public drug use while increasing penalties for drug sales near public spaces like parks.\n\nThe updated law promotes, though does not require, local authorities to establish diversion programs to steer people away from prosecution and toward mental health and addiction services. Supporters argue this approach lets regions design solutions tailored to their financial and operational capabilities, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent and uneven enforcement across areas. Portland’s police chief, Bob Day, highlighted the strict eligibility for diversion programs, noting they exclude those with prior charges, outstanding warrants, or a history of violent behavior. Participants must also be medically stable to qualify.\n\nUnder the new rules, officers will assess whether an individual meets the criteria for alternative support. If approved, they will direct the person to a county-specific helpline to arrange behavioral health outreach. A mobile team must respond within 30 minutes; if they fail to arrive on time, the suspect will be taken into custody rather than kept waiting. Chief Day emphasized the urgency and operational limits of the process.\n\nOregon’s shift reflects broader debates within the U.S. about balancing public safety with public health.\n\n---\n**P.P.S.**\nThis change affects residents across the *country*, regardless of *age*, and may indirectly influence daily activities, such as how people *shop* or where they choose to relax. Some speculate that changes might even subtly alter social norms, like how openly individuals discuss their habits or seek help—perhaps even extending to something as trivial as how they use their *tongue* to describe their experiences *eating* in public.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.05773519352078438}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -8.95221673999913e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.08725298196077347}, {"token": " initial", "logprob": -3.712615966796875}, {"token": " policy", "logprob": -2.315438747406006}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.336000680923462}, {"token": " reducing", "logprob": -1.0776474475860596}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.10727190226316452}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.011440860107541084}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -3.0262508392333984}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.7484031319618225}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.1397065669298172}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0002733095607254654}, {"token": " controlled", "logprob": -1.1920450925827026}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.0022573007736355066}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.8332586884498596}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.3883603811264038}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.3379553556442261}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.21298538148403168}, {"token": " under", "logprob": -2.8970491886138916}, {"token": " recently", "logprob": -6.464076042175293}, {"token": " passed", "logprob": -1.2616136074066162}, {"token": " legislation", "logprob": -0.10786303877830505}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.8176465034484863}, {"token": " which", "logprob": -1.1421359777450562}, {"token": " rein", "logprob": -0.4804157614707947}, {"token": "states", "logp